<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shwezigon Pagoda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1102">1102</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shwezigon Pagoda is a gilded Buddhist stupa at Nyaung-U near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagan">Bagan</a> in Myanmar, begun by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> of Pagan and completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a> around 1102. It is said to enshrine relics of the Buddha, and its bell-shaped form became the model for many later Burmese stupas. It remains an important place of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shwezigon-pagoda.jpg" alt="Shwezigon Pagoda" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DIMMIS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shwezigon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The chronicles of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> attribute the start of construction to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a>, founder of the Pagan Empire, in 1059-1060. According to legend, Anawrahta chose the site by sending out a white elephant carrying a relic of the Buddha, a frontal bone or a replica of the tooth relic, declaring that the pagoda would rise wherever it stopped. It stopped on a dune, hence the name Shwezigon, golden pagoda on a dune. Anawrahta built the lower terraces, and the rest of the structure is credited to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a>, who dedicated a stone pillar with Mon language inscriptions in the compound.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The stupa rises on five square terraces around a solid core, steep as a pyramid and topped with umbrellas, so that the whole appears as a cone. Stairways from the four cardinal directions lead up to an octagonal platform carrying the gilded stupa, and 550 glazed terracotta tiles showing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> are set into three of the terraces; there were once 584. Great chinthes guard the entrances, of which only the southern and western are in use.</p><p>Four bronze standing Buddhas, 12 to 13 feet high, face the four sides: Kakusandha to the north, Koṇāgamana to the east, Kassapa to the south and Gautama to the west. Below the Kassapa statue a pair of footprints with a chakra at their centre is carved on a sandstone slab. At the outer limits a shrine holds the 37 nats with a carved wooden figure of Thagyamin, king of the nats, believed to be 900 years old.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Worshippers believe the pagoda enshrines the Buddha's collar-bone and frontal bone from Prome and his tooth from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, although the relics have not been found and are believed stolen. Inside the solid body runs a maze of narrow passages where devotees fix dedicatory slabs on payment of a donation, praying for blessings. Earthquakes have damaged the pagoda many times; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> renovated it in the late 16th century, and after the 1975 earthquake it was strengthened with more than 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates given by devotees and gilded again in 1983-1984.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shwezigon Pagoda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kyansittha, who ruled from 1084 to c. 1113, consolidated the state, completed the <b>Shwezigon</b> stupa and built the Ananda temple.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
